# the target is a plain literal that nothing else has, right below Object.prototype, so a store into it defines. Each source whose keys are known is read field by field, and the literal is made once with all of it
# function merged(i) { return Object.assign({ c: 1 }, { a: i, b: 1 }); }   function tested(state: State) { return Object.assign({ c: 1 }, state); }   function other(target, i) { return Object.assign(target, { a: i, b: 1 }); }
# found by binding: the callee is the built-in itself
# the target is written as a NewObject here. The front end writes a literal whose members are all constants as a Data, which the form does not take: tests/answers/object-assign-into-a-literal-in-sight.ts, `constant`
# without TypedSlots the plan has no word for the order of `c` alone, so not even the allocation as written lists its field: the run with few mechanisms showed nothing
